<title>INDIA: BOMBAY DYEING SHUTS PLANT AFTER FIRE.</title>
<headline>BOMBAY DYEING SHUTS PLANT AFTER FIRE.</headline>
<dateline>BOMBAY 1997-08-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Indian textiles and petrochemicals firm Bombay Dyeing and Manufacturing Company Ltd has shut down its plant after a fire broke out on Saturday, an official said.</p>
<p>&quot;The cause of the fire is not known,&quot; a company official at the plant in Patalganga, about 100 kilometres (62.5 miles) from Bombay, said.</p>
<p>&quot;It destroyed some pipelines and equipments,&quot; he said. &quot;It might take days or weeks before production can be resumed.&quot;</p>
<p>He said the plant manufactured di-methyl terephthalate (DMT) and its raw material, paraxylene.</p>
<p>DMT is a raw material used in textiles.</p>
<p>--Bombay newsroom (91) 22-265-9500</p>
